## Migration Analysis

Your code has three patterns that need migration:

| Pattern | Location | Replacement |
|---------|----------|-------------|
| **Cancellable work loop** | `while (true)` in `RunLoop` | Check `CancellationToken` |
| **Blocking call interruption** | `_newEvent.WaitOne()` | `WaitHandle.WaitAny` with `token.WaitHandle` |
| **Thread.Interrupt + Thread.Abort** | `Stop()` | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |

## Key Pitfalls in This Code

1. **`Thread.Abort()` → throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** on .NET 6+
2. **`Thread.Interrupt()` → obsolete (`SYSLIB0046` in .NET 9)** and unreliable — it only works if the thread is in `WaitSleepJoin` state
3. **`ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` ignores cancellation** — you must use `WaitHandle.WaitAny` with the token's wait handle
4. **Race condition in original code** — multiple `Enqueue` calls before `RunLoop` wakes up will cause `_newEvent.Reset()` to clear the signal, but only one item is dequeued. The migrated version fixes this by draining the queue.

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly ManualResetEvent _newEvent = new ManualResetEvent(false);
    private readonly Queue<string> _queue = new Queue<string>();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
    private Thread? _thread;
    private readonly object _lock = new object();

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        var handles = new[] { _newEvent, _cts.Token.WaitHandle };

        while (!_cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // Blocks until either a new event arrives or cancellation is requested
            int signaled = WaitHandle.WaitAny(handles);

            if (signaled == 1) // cancellation token was signaled
                break;

            _newEvent.Reset();

            // Drain the queue to avoid losing items when multiple Enqueue
            // calls happen before RunLoop wakes up
            List<string> items;
            lock (_lock)
            {
                items = new List<string>(_queue);
                _queue.Clear();
            }

            foreach (var item in items)
            {
                if (_cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested)
                    break;
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        lock (_lock) { _queue.Enqueue(item); }
        _newEvent.Set();
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();           // signals the token → WaitAny returns immediately
        _thread?.Join(5000);     // wait for graceful exit
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts.Dispose();
        _newEvent.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## What Changed and Why

| Before (.NET Framework) | After (.NET 8) | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `while (true)` | `while (!_cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested)` | Cooperative cancellation replaces forcible abort |
| `_newEvent.WaitOne()` | `WaitHandle.WaitAny(handles)` | Unblocks on **either** new work or cancellation |
| `_thread.Interrupt()` | `_cts.Cancel()` | Token signal wakes `WaitAny` reliably |
| `_thread.Abort()` | *(removed)* | Not supported on .NET 6+; cooperative exit makes it unnecessary |
| No `IDisposable` | `IDisposable` added | `CancellationTokenSource` and `ManualResetEvent` must be disposed to avoid timer/handle leaks |
| Dequeue single item | Drain entire queue | Fixes race where multiple enqueues before wake lost items |

> **Note:** If `Handle()` is long-running, pass `_cts.Token` into it so cancellation is responsive even mid-processing. Cooperative cancellation only works if the code checks the token at regular intervals.
